# tests/test_loop_cost.bd - T15, CPU-load research programme.
#
# ONE QUESTION
#   What does a loop HEADER cost per iteration, over and above the
#   statements inside its body - and does `for ... in range(a, b)`
#   differ from `for ... in <list>` differ from an explicit `while`?
#
# WHY IT MATTERS
#   Every throttled loop in utils/ is priced as if only the body counted.
#   field_notes says "a hand-written loop costs real per-iteration ticks
#   even when the body only calls free-looking built-ins" but never put a
#   number on the header. Two corpus numbers refuse to fit 1 tick/line:
#     - test_cpu_decay.bd's idle iteration (cond + sleep(1) + log + incr)
#       measured a clean ~2.0s where 1-tick-per-line predicts ~1.3s:
#       ~7 ticks unaccounted for, per iteration.
#     - menu.bd's import phase ran 945 lines of module code in ~2.9s
#       (~29 ticks at base tier, not ~945).
#   If a loop pass costs 3 ticks, every loop estimate in field_notes is
#   3x understated. If it costs ~10, the whole pacing model is wrong.
#
# TWO BLOCKS, TWO CURRENCIES, ONE ANSWER
#   BLOCK 1 (METER, ~30s): tiny sub-lethal bursts, one get_cpu() capture
#     each. Reads the per-iteration cost off the CPU meter in points.
#       M0 floor (no work)          - the baseline every delta subtracts
#       M1 while x1  |  M2 while x2  |  M3 while x3   (the staircase)
#       MF for q in range(0,2)      - same 2 body units as M2
#       ML for v in lst2            - same 2 body units, list pre-built
#   BLOCK 2 (CLOCK, ~115s): 10 rounds x 6 body units per arm, paced by
#     sleep(2). Reads the same cost off the wall clock in seconds.
#       A  6 straight-line  a = a + 1        (no loop at all)
#       B  for q in range(0, 6):  a = a + 1
#       C  j = 0; while j < 6: a = a + 1; j = j + 1
#     Everything else - the outer while, sleep(2), r = r + 1, the two
#     timestamp reads, the single log() - is byte-identical across arms.
#
#   The two blocks cover each other's confound. The meter block cannot
#   resolve fractions of a tick; the clock block can, but arm B also
#   builds a range() once per round. MF - ML is exactly that range
#   construction cost, measured in the meter block, so arm B's number
#   can be corrected rather than caveated.
#
# WHAT EACH RESULT MEANS
#   headline numbers:  (M2 - M1)   points per while-iteration
#                      (spanB - spanA)  seconds per 60 for-iterations
#                      (spanC - spanA)  seconds per 60 while-iterations
#   Per-source-line charging (cond + body + incr each a tick):
#       M1~50  M2~80  M3 clamps at 100.  M2 - M1 = 30 points = 3 ticks.
#       spanB - spanA ~= 6s   spanC - spanA ~= 13s  at base tier.
#   Header charged once per LOOP, not per pass:
#       M2 - M1 ~= 20 points; spanB - spanA < 2s.
#   Header free AND increment free:
#       M2 - M1 ~= 10 points; spanB - spanA ~= 0; MF ~= M1.
#   Header much more expensive than a line (the ~2.0s-iteration model):
#       spanC - spanA > 30s, or arm C is KILLED mid-round. A kill in C
#       after A and B have printed is a RESULT, not a failure: it puts
#       the while pass at >= ~5 ticks and prices the header on its own.
#   Not a clean multiple of the meter's step size -> per-OPERATION
#       charging, and every line-count estimate in the corpus is void.
#
# INTERNAL VALIDITY CHECKS - if any fails, discard the run
#   a= must read  1 / 3 / 6 / 8 / 10  at the ends of M1/M2/M3/MF/ML.
#     (MF reading 11 instead of 10 means range(0,2) is INCLUSIVE - a
#      real finding in itself, and it re-scales arm B by 7/6.)
#   a= must read  60 / 120 / 180  at the ends of arms A / B / C.
#   rounds= must read 10 in all three arms.
#   M3 - M2 should equal M2 - M1 unless M3 has clamped at 100.
#   CROSS-CALIBRATION: (M2 - M1) / (spanC - spanA) x 60 must equal the
#     meter's points-per-second. If the two blocks disagree, one of the
#     two instruments is nonlinear - report that, it outranks the answer.
#
# SAFETY / TICK BUDGET (base tier, 10 ticks/sec, kill = 5s at 100%)
#   Largest uninterrupted burst under the 1-tick-per-line model is arm
#   C's round at ~20 ticks (~2.0s) - well inside the window. Every round
#   opens with sleep(2), and a sleeping script does not accumulate the
#   kill streak (test_decode_pacing.bd logged cpu=100 for 840s straight
#   without dying). Arms ascend cheapest-first so a kill still leaves
#   every cheaper arm measured. No get_cpu() is ever inside a measured
#   span or inside any loop - no feedback path.
#
# HOW TO RUN
#   Fresh session, `ps` and `jobs` empty, nothing else run yet. Then:
#     run test_loop_cost
#   Touch nothing for ~2.5 minutes. Paste back EVERY line starting
#   "T15". Run it TWICE. If (M2 - M1) disagrees between the two runs by
#   more than one meter step, run a third time.
#
# READING IT AFTERWARDS
#   ticks per for-iteration   = (spanB - spanA) / 60 / sec_per_tick
#                               minus (MF - ML) / 10 / 6  (range setup)
#   ticks per while-iteration = (spanC - spanA) / 60 / sec_per_tick - 1
#                               (the -1 removes the explicit j = j + 1)
#   Multiply every loop-based cost estimate in field_notes by that.

var t0 = get_unix_time()
var a = 0
var j = 0
var r = 0
var c = 0
var mark = 0
var span = 0
var lst2 = [0, 1]
log("T15 start - loop iteration cost; meter block then clock block")

# ============ BLOCK 1 - METER ============

# M0: floor. No work at all. Every later delta subtracts this.
sleep(3)
c = get_cpu()
log("T15 M0 floor cpu=" + str(c) + " a=" + str(a) + " t=" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))

# M1: while, 1 iteration.
sleep(3)
j = 0
while j < 1:
    a = a + 1
    j = j + 1
c = get_cpu()
log("T15 M1 while1 cpu=" + str(c) + " a=" + str(a) + " t=" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))

# M2: while, 2 iterations. M2 - M1 IS one while-iteration in points.
sleep(3)
j = 0
while j < 2:
    a = a + 1
    j = j + 1
c = get_cpu()
log("T15 M2 while2 cpu=" + str(c) + " a=" + str(a) + " t=" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))

# M3: while, 3 iterations. Linearity check; expected to clamp at 100.
sleep(3)
j = 0
while j < 3:
    a = a + 1
    j = j + 1
c = get_cpu()
log("T15 M3 while3 cpu=" + str(c) + " a=" + str(a) + " t=" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))

# MF: for over range(), 2 body units. Compare against M2.
sleep(3)
for q in range(0, 2):
    a = a + 1
c = get_cpu()
log("T15 MF forrange2 cpu=" + str(c) + " a=" + str(a) + " t=" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))

# ML: for over a PRE-BUILT list, 2 body units. MF - ML = range() setup.
sleep(3)
for v in lst2:
    a = a + 1
c = get_cpu()
log("T15 ML forlist2 cpu=" + str(c) + " a=" + str(a) + " t=" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))

# ============ BLOCK 2 - CLOCK ============

log("T15 clock block begins - arms A straight6, B forrange6, C while6")
sleep(3)
a = 0

# ARM A: 6 straight-line units per round, no loop at all. The baseline.
log("T15 A-begin straight6 x10 rounds")
sleep(3)
r = 0
mark = get_unix_time()
while r < 10:
    sleep(2)
    r = r + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
    a = a + 1
span = get_unix_time() - mark
c = get_cpu()
log("T15 A straight6 span=" + str(span) + " rounds=" + str(r) + " a=" + str(a) + " cpu=" + str(c) + " t=" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))

# ARM B: identical work, wrapped in a for over range(). B - A = 60 for-passes.
log("T15 B-begin forrange6 x10 rounds - if this is the last line, the for arm's burst was lethal")
sleep(3)
r = 0
mark = get_unix_time()
while r < 10:
    sleep(2)
    r = r + 1
    for q in range(0, 6):
        a = a + 1
span = get_unix_time() - mark
c = get_cpu()
log("T15 B forrange6 span=" + str(span) + " rounds=" + str(r) + " a=" + str(a) + " cpu=" + str(c) + " t=" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))

# ARM C: identical work, explicit while. C - A = 60 while-passes + 60 increments.
log("T15 C-begin while6 x10 rounds - if this is the last line, the while arm's burst was lethal")
sleep(3)
r = 0
mark = get_unix_time()
while r < 10:
    sleep(2)
    r = r + 1
    j = 0
    while j < 6:
        a = a + 1
        j = j + 1
span = get_unix_time() - mark
c = get_cpu()
log("T15 C while6 span=" + str(span) + " rounds=" + str(r) + " a=" + str(a) + " cpu=" + str(c) + " t=" + str(get_unix_time() - t0))

log("T15 done - headline numbers are (M2-M1), (spanB-spanA), (spanC-spanA); a must be 180")
